# Next.js Styling Rules

## TailwindCSS Requirements

- **ALL styling MUST go through TailwindCSS utility classes**
- Never use inline `style` attributes for styling (exception: dynamic values that require calculation)
- Never create custom CSS files for component-specific styles
- Never use `@apply` directives in component files (only allowed in `globals.css` for base layer)
- Never use CSS modules (`.module.css` files)
- Never use styled-components or CSS-in-JS libraries
- Use TailwindCSS v4 utility classes exclusively

## Cohesive Styling Choices

**CRITICAL**: Always prioritize cohesive styling that fits with existing patterns in the codebase.

### Before Adding New Styles

1. **Review existing components** - Look at similar components in the codebase to understand established patterns
2. **Check spacing conventions** - Note the spacing scale used (e.g., `p-4`, `gap-6`, `mb-6`)
3. **Match border radius** - Use the same border radius values (`rounded-lg`, `rounded-md`, etc.)
4. **Follow typography patterns** - Match text sizes (`text-sm`, `text-base`, `text-xl`, etc.) and font weights
5. **Align color usage** - Use the same semantic color choices for similar elements
6. **Match interactive states** - Use consistent hover/active/focus states (`hover:bg-primary/90`, etc.)
7. **Follow layout patterns** - Match flex/grid patterns, gaps, and alignment

### Consistency Checklist

When adding or modifying styles:

- [ ] Reviewed similar existing components for patterns
- [ ] Matched spacing scale used elsewhere (`p-4`, `gap-6`, etc.)
- [ ] Used consistent border radius values
- [ ] Matched typography sizes and weights
- [ ] Used semantic colors consistently with existing code
- [ ] Applied consistent interactive states (hover, focus, active)
- [ ] Followed existing responsive breakpoint patterns
- [ ] Matched card/layout patterns where applicable

### Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Matches existing patterns
// If other cards use: className="mb-6"
<Card className="mb-6">
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle className="text-xl">Title</CardTitle>
  </CardHeader>
</Card>

// ❌ Bad - Creates inconsistent spacing
// If other cards use: className="mb-6"
<Card className="mb-8">  // Different spacing breaks cohesion
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle className="text-2xl">Title</CardTitle>  // Different size breaks cohesion
  </CardHeader>
</Card>
```

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Matches existing icon styling
// If other icons use: className="h-5 w-5 text-primary"
<BarChart3 className="h-5 w-5 text-primary" />

// ❌ Bad - Creates inconsistent icon styling
<BarChart3 className="h-6 w-6 text-blue-500" />  // Different size and color breaks cohesion
```

## Color Usage - Semantic Colors Only

**CRITICAL**: Always use semantic color names instead of explicit color names like red, green, blue, etc.

### Available Semantic Colors

Use these semantic color names from the theme:

- `primary` / `primary-foreground` - Main brand color
- `secondary` / `secondary-foreground` - Secondary brand color
- `accent` / `accent-foreground` - Accent/highlight color
- `destructive` / `destructive-foreground` - Error/danger states
- `muted` / `muted-foreground` - Muted/subdued elements
- `background` / `foreground` - Base background and text
- `card` / `card-foreground` - Card components
- `popover` / `popover-foreground` - Popover components
- `border` - Border colors
- `input` - Input border colors
- `ring` - Focus ring colors

### ❌ FORBIDDEN Color Usage

Do NOT use explicit color names like:

- `bg-red-500`, `text-green-600`, `border-blue-400`
- `bg-[#ff0000]`, `text-[rgb(0,255,0)]`
- Any Tailwind color palette names (red, green, blue, yellow, etc.) unless absolutely necessary for non-semantic purposes

### ✅ CORRECT Color Usage Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Using semantic colors
<div className="bg-primary text-primary-foreground">
<button className="bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground">
<div className="border border-border bg-card text-card-foreground">
<span className="text-muted-foreground">Subdued text</span>
<button className="bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground">Delete</button>

// ❌ Bad - Using explicit colors
<div className="bg-blue-500 text-white">
<button className="bg-red-600 hover:bg-red-700 text-white">
<div className="border border-gray-300 bg-white text-black">
```

## Styling Patterns

### Background and Text Colors

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<div className="bg-background text-foreground">
<div className="bg-primary text-primary-foreground">
<div className="bg-muted text-muted-foreground">

// ❌ Bad
<div className="bg-white text-black">
<div className="bg-blue-500 text-white">
```

### Borders

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<div className="border border-border">
<input className="border border-input">

// ❌ Bad
<div className="border border-gray-300">
<input className="border border-[#ccc]">
```

### Interactive States

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<button className="bg-primary hover:bg-primary/90 active:bg-primary/80">
<button className="bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground">
<button className="ring-2 ring-ring focus:ring-ring">

// ❌ Bad
<button className="bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-600">
<button className="ring-2 ring-blue-500">
```

### Muted/Secondary Elements

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<span className="text-muted-foreground">Helper text</span>
<div className="bg-muted text-muted-foreground">Secondary content</div>

// ❌ Bad
<span className="text-gray-500">Helper text</span>
<div className="bg-gray-100 text-gray-600">Secondary content</div>
```

## Exception Cases

### When Explicit Colors Are Acceptable

Only use explicit Tailwind colors in these rare cases:

1. **Charts/Data Visualization** - When displaying data that requires distinct colors (e.g., `chart-1`, `chart-2` colors from theme)
2. **Non-semantic decorative elements** - Patterns, backgrounds that don't convey meaning
3. **Third-party component overrides** - When modifying third-party components that don't support semantic colors

Even in these cases, prefer theme colors like `chart-1`, `chart-2`, etc. when available.

## Spacing and Layout

- Use Tailwind's spacing scale: `p-4`, `m-2`, `gap-4`, etc.
- Use Tailwind's responsive breakpoints: `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`, `2xl:`
- Use Tailwind's flexbox/grid utilities: `flex`, `grid`, `items-center`, `justify-between`

## Responsive Design

Always use Tailwind's responsive prefixes:

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<div className="p-4 md:p-6 lg:p-8">
<div className="text-sm md:text-base lg:text-lg">
<div className="flex-col md:flex-row">

// ❌ Bad
<div style={{ padding: '1rem' }}> // Don't use inline styles
```

## Dark Mode Support

Semantic colors automatically support dark mode through CSS variables defined in `globals.css`. No additional code needed - colors will switch automatically based on the `.dark` class on the root element.

## Component Styling Checklist

Before committing any component with styling:

- [ ] Reviewed existing similar components for styling patterns
- [ ] Matched spacing, typography, and layout patterns from existing code
- [ ] All styles use Tailwind utility classes
- [ ] No inline `style` attributes (except dynamic calculations)
- [ ] No custom CSS files created
- [ ] All colors use semantic names (primary, secondary, accent, etc.)
- [ ] No explicit color names (red, blue, green, etc.) used
- [ ] Responsive classes use Tailwind breakpoints
- [ ] Dark mode works automatically (test with `.dark` class)
- [ ] Consistent with existing component patterns (icons, cards, buttons, etc.)

## Component Examples

### ✅ Correct Component Styling

```tsx
export function Button({ children, variant = 'primary' }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      className={cn(
        "px-4 py-2 rounded-md font-medium transition-colors",
        variant === 'primary' && "bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/90",
        variant === 'secondary' && "bg-secondary text-secondary-foreground hover:bg-secondary/90",
        variant === 'destructive' && "bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground hover:bg-destructive/90"
      )}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

### ❌ Incorrect Component Styling

```tsx
// ❌ Bad - Using explicit colors
export function Button({ children }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      className="px-4 py-2 rounded-md bg-blue-500 text-white hover:bg-blue-600"
      style={{ padding: '0.5rem' }} // ❌ Inline styles
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - Using CSS modules
import styles from './Button.module.css';
export function Button({ children }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button className={styles.button}>{children}</button>;
}
```

